{
  "note": "Lateral Inhibition and Adaptation",
  "source": "e54f4f9cee75",
  "script": "d00b23b75722",
  "voice": "Jessica Gallagher - Clear and Neutral",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "b5fbcd7d2e06",
  "duration": 200.751,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Lateral Inhibition and Adaptation",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Lateral inhibition and adaptation are two coding strategies of the somatosensory system.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 5.68
        },
        {
          "text": "Lateral inhibition enhances the quality of sensory information by suppressing the pathways that flank the most active one.",
          "start": 6.16,
          "end": 13.32
        },
        {
          "text": "Adaptation, on the other hand, codes for stimulus duration.",
          "start": 13.52,
          "end": 17.36
        },
        {
          "text": "It does so through the way receptor firing declines, or persists, during a sustained stimulus.",
          "start": 17.6,
          "end": 23.36
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll work through lateral inhibition first, then adaptation and its two classes of receptor, before a brief summary.",
          "start": 23.84,
          "end": 31.12
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "1. Lateral inhibition",
      "start": 31.974,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Picture a localised stimulus, for instance a pin on the skin.",
          "start": 31.974,
          "end": 36.587
        },
        {
          "text": "It activates several primary sensory neurons at once, and the response of each one is proportional to stimulus strength.",
          "start": 36.641,
          "end": 44.254
        },
        {
          "text": "So the pathway closest to the stimulus fires the most, its immediate neighbours fire less, and distant ones remain at their tonic level.",
          "start": 44.294,
          "end": 53.134
        },
        {
          "text": "At the relay, the pathway closest to the stimulus inhibits its neighbours.",
          "start": 53.174,
          "end": 57.947
        },
        {
          "text": "In doing so, it pushes the flanking pathways below that tonic level.",
          "start": 58.001,
          "end": 62.481
        },
        {
          "text": "The effect is contrast enhancement.",
          "start": 62.507,
          "end": 65.201
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, inhibition of the lateral neurons enhances perception of the stimulus.",
          "start": 65.227,
          "end": 70.734
        },
        {
          "text": "That happens because the difference between the strongly active central pathway and its silenced surroundings is exaggerated.",
          "start": 70.774,
          "end": 78.094
        },
        {
          "text": "This is how lateral inhibition sharpens the quality of sensory information.",
          "start": 78.134,
          "end": 83.014
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Adaptation",
      "start": 83.775,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now we turn to adaptation, which describes how the receptor potential and the firing rate evolve while a stimulus is maintained.",
          "start": 83.775,
          "end": 92.122
        },
        {
          "text": "It is the code for stimulus duration.",
          "start": 92.148,
          "end": 94.855
        },
        {
          "text": "Receptors split into two classes by their temporal response, namely rapidly adapting and slowly adapting.",
          "start": 94.895,
          "end": 102.335
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "3. Rapidly adapting receptors",
      "start": 103.105,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Let's take the rapidly adapting class first.",
          "start": 103.105,
          "end": 106.265
        },
        {
          "text": "Here, the receptor potential decreases in response to a sustained stimulus.",
          "start": 106.305,
          "end": 111.625
        },
        {
          "text": "As a result, the afferent produces only an on-off burst of action potentials at the start of stimulation and again at its end, and then falls silent.",
          "start": 111.665,
          "end": 122.105
        },
        {
          "text": "For this reason, these receptors convey information on changes in ongoing stimulation, rather than on the steady stimulus itself.",
          "start": 122.145,
          "end": 130.745
        },
        {
          "text": "The classic example is the Pacinian corpuscle.",
          "start": 130.785,
          "end": 134.145
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Slowly adapting receptors",
      "start": 134.922,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Slowly adapting receptors behave the opposite way.",
          "start": 134.922,
          "end": 138.522
        },
        {
          "text": "They keep firing, with sustained activity for as long as the stimulus is present.",
          "start": 138.602,
          "end": 143.842
        },
        {
          "text": "This makes them suited to convey information on the spatial attributes of the stimulus, such as its size and shape.",
          "start": 143.882,
          "end": 150.789
        },
        {
          "text": "The example given for this class is nociceptors.",
          "start": 150.815,
          "end": 154.202
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 154.958,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To sum up, lateral inhibition enhances the quality of sensory information.",
          "start": 154.958,
          "end": 160.371
        },
        {
          "text": "The pathway closest to the stimulus inhibits its neighbours, which exaggerates contrast and sharpens perception of the stimulus.",
          "start": 160.425,
          "end": 168.451
        },
        {
          "text": "Adaptation, meanwhile, codes for stimulus duration.",
          "start": 168.505,
          "end": 172.438
        },
        {
          "text": "Rapidly adapting receptors, like the Pacinian corpuscle, lose their receptor potential during a sustained stimulus.",
          "start": 172.478,
          "end": 179.638
        },
        {
          "text": "They fire only an on-off burst of action potentials, signalling changes in ongoing stimulation.",
          "start": 179.678,
          "end": 186.518
        },
        {
          "text": "Slowly adapting receptors, like nociceptors, fire throughout the stimulus and signal its spatial attributes, such as size and shape.",
          "start": 186.558,
          "end": 195.331
        },
        {
          "text": "With that, you have both coding strategies, one for contrast and one for duration.",
          "start": 195.385,
          "end": 200.798
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
